  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Interesting synoptic setup with vertically-stacked low \npressure system centered across Indiana early this morning. A \npositive tilt mid-level trough extends newd from the closed low \nacross sern Michigan into sern Ontario. This positive tilt trough \nshould serve as a slow-moving focus for convergence and deformation, \nwith ongoing showers and embedded convective activity likely to \nspread northeastward during the daylight hours today (Sunday). While \nbest precipitation focus should be across the International Border \ninto Ontario, may see some bands of moderate to locally heavy \nrainfall lifting across the St. Lawrence Valley later this morning \ninto the afternoon hours. Combination of rich moisture (PW values \n1.9 to 2.0) and some training thunderstorm potential with SBCAPE \nvalues 500-1000 J/kg does bring potential for widespread 1-2\" \nrainfall with locally higher amounts across St. Lawrence and \nFranklin Counties today. The 00Z HREF Probability Matched Mean QPF \nguidance suggest a few locations could see rainfall amts exceeding \n2\" thru 12Z Monday. Topographically, it is relatively difficult to \nget flash flooding in the St. Lawrence Valley given flat terrain, \nand QPF values suggest only an isolated threat. Will continue to \nmessage this isolated threat via the Hazardous Weather Outlook. \nNarrow CAPE profiles with modest mid-level lapse rates suggest there \nisn't a severe threat with this activity. Have included heavy \nrainfall wording only into the forecast grids.  \n\nAbundant clouds across the St. Lawrence Valley and the Adirondacks \nwill keep high temperatures generally in the 74-78F range this \nafternoon. Rain generally holds off across Vermont thru early \nevening, and partial sunshine allows temperatures to reach the lower \n80s in most locations from the Champlain Valley ewd. \n\nFor tonight and Monday, wave low moving newd across the Eastern \nGreat Lakes and across the St. Lawrence Valley will eventually allow \nthe baroclinic zone to shift ewd as as cold front tonight into \nMonday. It appears the bulk of the widespread shower and embedded \nthunderstorm activity will be 06-18Z Monday from the Champlain \nValley ewd associated with strengthening low-level frontal \nconvergence and right entrance region of impressive 90-100kt 250mb \njet for this time of the year. Have indicated 80-100% PoPs overnight \ninto Monday morning, gradually diminishing thereafter as best \ndynamic support and PWATs shift east of our forecast area. Overall \nrainfall amounts from the Champlain Valley ewd are expected to range \nfrom 0.5\" to 1\", with some locally heavier amounts possible due to \nthunderstorms. Flash flooding is not currently expected in VT. Highs \non Monday will generally range from the upper 70s to lower 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A typical mid to late summer set-up is in store for the \nsecond half of the upcoming week and next weekend. A strong Bermuda \nhigh pressure system (nearly 600 dm) is forecasted to situate across \nthe western Atlantic with an elongated but not highly amplified \ntrough across the northern US. This will keep the longwave pattern \nThursday through Sunday relatively the same with several rounds of \nshortwave energy aiding the longwave trough from falling apart. The \nNorth Country will be in a peculiar spot between the ridge and main \nupper level trough which should help bring warmth, moisture, and the \noccasional shortwave trough overhead. Basically, this means that we \nshould see periods of showers and possibly a few thunderstorms daily \nwith temperatures hovering within a few degrees of normal (generally \nlow to mid 80s). No significant storm systems appear likely on the \nhorizon given we have no distinguishable surface features to produce \nconvergence/lift and modest flow aloft. High PWAT values in the 90+ \npercent range will allow any showers or thunderstorms that develop \nto be heavy rainers but they will be scattered at best and short-\nlived, meaning flooding isn't a concern at this time.",
